%S A401902 1,4,1,9,1,12,1,23,1,4,1,24,1,27,1,52,1,4,1,9,1,43,1,52,1,4,1,12,1,83,
%T A401902 1,80,1,4,1,9,1,73,1,86,1,4,1,15,1,85,1,162,1,4,1,9,1,127,1,16,1,4,1,
%U A401902 20,1,120,1,147,1,4,1,9,1,27,1,228,1,4,1,17,1,12,1,201,1,4,1,9,1,158,1,183
%N A401902 Number of sites that topple when the 2n-th grain is added at the origin of an initially empty sandpile on the first quadrant, where a site (x, y) with at least 2 grains topples by sending one grain to each of (x+1, y) and (x, y+1).
%C A401902 Grains are added one at a time, and the pile is stabilized after each grain; the topplings caused by one grain form an avalanche. The odd-numbered grains cause no topplings, so the number of sites that topple after the N-th grain is a(N/2) for even N and 0 for odd N. Equivalently, a(n) is the number of sites that topple when the grains are added in pairs and the n-th pair arrives.
%C A401902 Each site topples at most once per avalanche, so a(n) is also the number of topplings caused by the 2n-th grain. On each layer x + y = d, the sites that topple form an interval centered on the line x = y. The number of these sites is 1 on layer 0 and changes by exactly 1 from each layer to the next until it reaches 0. Compare Theorems 6.1 and 6.2 of Inagaki et al.
%C A401902 Let L(n) be the number of layers that contain a site that topples. Then L(n) is odd, and for each k >= 1, exactly A000108(k-1) of any 2^(2*k-1) consecutive values of n have L(n) = 2*k-1. The ratio A000108(k-1)/2^(2*k-1) is the probability that a simple random walk started at 1 first reaches 0 at step 2*k-1. If L(n) = 2*k-1, then a(n) == k (mod 2).
%C A401902 As in the randomly driven two-dimensional directed sandpile of Dhar and Ramaswamy, each site topples at most once per avalanche and the toppled sites on each layer form an interval. There its two ends perform random walks, and an avalanche topples sites on exactly T layers with probability A000108(T)/2^(2*T+1) (Dhar 1999, Section 7, eq. (45)). Here all configurations are symmetric about the line x = y, so the two ends move together, and this probability is the exact proportion of n with L(n) = 2*T+1 in every window of 2^(2*T+1) consecutive values of n.
%C A401902 In the avalanches of any 2^d consecutive values of n, each site (x, y) with x + y = d topples exactly binomial(d, x) times. For n = 1..2^d this follows from Theorem 5.1 of Inagaki, Khovanova and Luo, applied to 2^(d+1) chips placed at the origin at once, and the operator relations of the abelian sandpile (Dhar 1999, Section 3.3) extend it to every window.
%C A401902 For each v >= 1, the set of n with a(n) = v is periodic with period dividing 2^v. In contrast, the n with a(n) odd have the irrational density sqrt(2)/2 (see Formula), so a(n) mod 2 is not eventually periodic.
%C A401902 On the directed binary tree, the same rule with grains added at the root makes A038712(n) sites topple at the 2n-th grain (Krawczyk et al.).
%C A401902 See the Ferudun link for proofs.
%H A401902 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A401902/b401902.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A401902 Deepak Dhar, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/cond-mat/9909009">Studying Self-Organized Criticality with Exactly Solved Models</a>, arXiv:cond-mat/9909009 [cond-mat.stat-mech], 1999. See Sections 3.3 and 7.
%H A401902 Deepak Dhar and Ramakrishna Ramaswamy, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.63.1659">Exactly solved model of self-organized critical phenomena</a>, Phys. Rev. Lett. 63 (1989), 1659-1662.
%H A401902 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A401902/a401902.txt">Proofs of the statements in the comments and formulas</a>.
%H A401902 Ryota Inagaki, Tanya Khovanova, and Austin Luo, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.09125">Chip-firing on the Lattice of Nonnegative Integer Points</a>, arXiv:2601.09125 [math.CO], 2026. See Lemma 2.3 and Theorems 5.1, 6.1 and 6.2 of v2.
%H A401902 Malgorzata J. Krawczyk, Paweł Oświęcimka, Krzysztof Kułakowski, and Stanisław Drożdż, <a href="https://doi.org/10.3390/e21100968">Ordered Avalanches on the Bethe Lattice</a>, Entropy 21 (2019), 968.
%F A401902 a(2*n-1) = 1.
%F A401902 Sum_{j=1..n} a(j) = T(2*n), where T(N) is the number of topplings needed to stabilize N grains placed at the origin at once.
%F A401902 Sum_{j=1..2^m} a(j) = A389565(m+1) for m >= 0 (see Inagaki et al., Lemma 2.3).
%F A401902 Limit_{N->oo} (1/N)*#{n <= N: a(n) is odd} = Sum_{j>=0} A000108(2*j)/2^(4*j+1) = sqrt(2)/2 (A010503).
%F A401902 Limit_{N->oo} (1/N)*#{n <= N: a(2*n) is odd} = sqrt(2) - 1 (A188582).
%e A401902 a(1) = 1: the 2nd grain makes the origin topple, and (1, 0) and (0, 1) receive one grain each.
%e A401902 a(2) = 4: the 4th grain makes the origin topple; then (1, 0) and (0, 1) topple, and (1, 1) receives two grains and topples. The numbers of sites that topple on the layers x + y = 0, 1, 2 are 1, 2, 1, so L(2) = 3.
%e A401902 For n = 4 and n = 6 (the 8th and 12th grains), the numbers of sites that topple on the layers x + y = 0, 1, 2, ... are 1, 2, 3, 2, 1 and 1, 2, 1, 2, 3, 2, 1. So a(4) = 9 with L(4) = 5 = 2*3-1, and a(6) = 12 with L(6) = 7 = 2*4-1; indeed 9 == 3 and 12 == 4 (mod 2).
%e A401902 Among n = 1..32, exactly A000108(2) = 2 values have L(n) = 5, namely n = 4 and n = 20.
%e A401902 The n with a(n) = 4 are those with n == 2 (mod 8), and the n with a(n) = 9 are those with n == 4 (mod 16).
%o A401902 (Python)
%o A401902 from collections import defaultdict, deque
%o A401902 h = defaultdict(int)
%o A401902 for n in range(1, 89):
%o A401902     h[0, 0] += 2
%o A401902     q = deque([(0, 0)])
%o A401902     toppled = set()
%o A401902     while q:
%o A401902         x, y = q.popleft()
%o A401902         while h[x, y] >= 2:
%o A401902             h[x, y] -= 2
%o A401902             toppled.add((x, y))
%o A401902             for p in ((x + 1, y), (x, y + 1)):
%o A401902                 h[p] += 1
%o A401902                 if h[p] == 2:
%o A401902                     q.append(p)
%o A401902     print(len(toppled))
%Y A401902 Cf. A000108, A010503, A038712, A188582, A352226, A389565, A390129, A390355.
